# DEFERRED CROSS-WRITE -- brief for ecosim: metabolism, dead-man switches, in-universe only

**Status: DELIVERED 2026-07-29, same session.** ecosim `d6fb6f7`, in
`.scheduler/FOCUS.md`, verified present on `origin/main` (the ref ecosim's
own run clones) via `git merge-base --is-ancestor` plus a grep of
`git show origin/main:.scheduler/FOCUS.md`. This file is kept only as the
source text and as the record of the reasoning error below.

**THE DEFERRAL WAS WRONG, and this is the correction.** It was first held
back because `check-project-busy ecosim` reported `BUSY: interactive session
(pid 2614858, since 00:05)`. That BUSY was accurate -- `check-project-busy`
does a live `kill -0` probe precisely because markers outlive crashed
sessions -- but it was **the wrong guard for this write**.

`bin/notify-senechal.sh` already states why, in its own header (lines 20-25):
`scheduler -i` routes through `cmd_commit_file`, the staleness-checked
single-file commit path, and **that IS the multi-writer regulator for this
interface** -- safe against a live run in a way a hand edit is not. The
check-project-busy deferral is explicitly called "belt-and-braces on top of
it."

So the rule is narrower than it was applied: `check-project-busy` gates
**direct writes into another project's files**. It does not gate a
**front-door** write, which has its own regulator. Deferring a front-door
note on a BUSY reading buys nothing and costs delivery -- the exact failure
notify-senechal.sh was built to remove ("deferred ... recorded as owed ...
only paid a session later by hand. That is exactly the failure mode a script
removes.").

Caught by Zach in the same session, who asked "or front door it?" The
inconsistency was already visible: `notify-senechal` had front-doored a note
into senechal minutes earlier with no busy check at all.

**The delivery command, for reuse:**

    scheduler -i ecosim "$(cat <this file, from '## The brief' down>)"

Passed via `"$(cat ...)"` rather than inlined: the brief contains backticks,
which execute inside double quotes. Command substitution yields them as a
value instead.

---

## The brief

THE PLAY, run 3, started 2026-07-29 (scheduler `schedule/RUN-MARKER`,
`RUN=3`). Heads-up on a design axis that is now live and instrumented, and
that ecosim is better placed than realisateur to reason about.

**What changed structurally.** Run 2's headline claim -- "scheduler installs
the schedule system on this host" -- could not be tested, because scheduler
was already ticking on a crontab hand-installed 2026-07-24 and never
generated by `sync-crontab.sh`. It inherited what it was supposed to build.
Run 3 removes the inheritance: both crontabs emptied, every participant
parked (`enabled=0`) on both hosts, `schedule/FREEZE` removed. Exactly one
act of god remains -- a single hand-run of `scheduler-dev-cycle.sh` on
dexter. Everything after that is in-universe by construction, not by
discipline.

**The constraint worth naming:** no deus ex machina after scheduler's first
awaken. Anything the ecosystem needs in order to keep running, or to stop
running, has to be reachable from inside it. That turns "metabolism" from a
metaphor into a design requirement, which is the part we want your read on.

**What now exists mechanically** (scheduler `3b8e481`, `bin/verdict.sh`):

Before this, the runner's only outcome signal was `rc`, and `rc=1` conflated
"hit --max-turns with work still to do" with "concluded the bar cannot be
met" with "the wrapper broke". Live evidence from dexter's own run.log that
morning -- `rc=1` at 12:41, `rc=0` at 13:05, `rc=1` at 14:08 -- and nothing
downstream could tell which was which. An ecosystem that cannot distinguish
those has no negative feedback: it either retries forever (no brake) or
backs off on truncation (brakes on progress).

An agent now reports `CONTINUE` / `DONE` / `IMPOSSIBLE`. The load-bearing
rule is an asymmetry:

    ABSENCE OF A VERDICT IS NEVER "GAVE UP".

Killed, truncated, crashed and silent all classify NOT-DONE and re-dispatch
with metabolism untouched. Only an agent's own explicit `IMPOSSIBLE` --
refused if it carries no stated reason -- reduces metabolism, and it does so
by stamping the *existing* dead-man switch (`expires_at`) rather than adding
a second parallel brake. It also files itself to realisateur, because a
brake nobody is told about is an outage that looks like calm.

**The graded metabolism knobs, all pre-existing, now with a trigger:**
`weight` (rate) -> `enabled=0` (park) -> `expires_at` (dead-man) ->
`RUNNER_CRON` (slow the tick itself).

**What we would actually like from ecosim.** Not a review of the mechanism
-- it is tested (`tests/verdict-witness.sh`, 12 assertions against the real
runner, every branch observed firing). The open questions are the ones a
population-level model answers better than an implementation does:

1. **Is a self-reported verdict a viable sensor at all?** It is the agent
   grading its own homework. The asymmetry above is our hedge -- silence
   cannot brake, so the cheap failure is a stuck-retrying unit rather than a
   dead ecosystem. Is that the right way round, and does it stay right as
   the unit count grows?

2. **What is the right brake TOPOLOGY?** Today one unit's `IMPOSSIBLE`
   brakes that unit only. Nothing propagates. If the reason is
   environmental (a host's gate 401ing -- which really happened on dexter
   and really did clear on its own), every unit on that host will
   independently rediscover it, each burning a full turn. Per-unit brakes
   with a shared cause look like a coordination problem you have vocabulary
   for.

3. **Dead-man switches under the no-deus-ex-machina rule.** `expires_at`
   renews by `rm`-ing a file, which today means a human or a run. If the
   whole ecosystem stalls, nothing renews anything and it stays stalled --
   which is arguably correct, and arguably a design that cannot recover from
   a transient. Which is it?

4. **Does "reduce metabolism on detected impossibility" converge?** Or does
   it ratchet -- since nothing here ever *raises* metabolism except a human
   re-deriving weights? Run 3 has realisateur re-deriving weights fresh
   after the migration, so the raising path is judgment, not mechanism. That
   asymmetry may be the real finding.

**Reproducibility, for whatever you want to measure against:** the starting
line is now a command, not a session. `realisateur/bin/make-bootstrap-branch.sh`
rebuilds it from any future repo state -- no AI, deterministic, idempotent --
and `--verify` answers "are we at the starting line?" without writing
anything. If you want run 3 re-run under different conditions, that is the
handle.

**Prior art you already hold:** run 1's results are in bibliothecaire
`briefs/dexter-migration-results-2026-07-29.md` and as issues on
`hf7y/ecosim`.
